From: Randy Mace <aa160@ra.cs.OhioU.Edu>
Date: Wed, 7 Sep 1994 21:20:37 -0400 (EDT)
Subject: [#94-9-54] FROM THE LISTOWNER (ACT) (fwd)

The author, noting a "dangerous trend" in the citation post, said,

(deletions)

> There has always been a paramilitary faction, but it is now mushrooming far
> beyond in 

(I suspect the author meant "beyond the")

> normal ultra-racist, knuckle dragging circles

Labeling by association can usually be counted on to lose friends and
alienate people.

> into more mainstream, normally law abiding conservative-libertarian-
> constitutionalist circles. 

> People seem to be forgetting enmasse that war for the future is mainly a
> battle of ideas and are reaching for their guns in response to the
> leftward impulse of the Clinton Administration. 

Clinton, et al, certainly seem to have forgotten Mao Tse Tung's quotation
that "power emenates from the barrel of a gun" (my apologies if that is
not absolutely accurately quoted) and fail to notice that their policies end
up concentrating power in the hands of the government and the criminals.  

I suggest to the author that all that is being observed in the cited post
and its ilk are the ravings of borderline psychotics - who will always be with
us - feeling emboldened to speak out by the pro-gun/anti-gun rhetoric
surrounding the "Crime" Bill.

There are many more of us who stand by the Second Amendment (because it is,
ultimately, the guarantor of all of the others) who are not the least
inclined to use our weapons to defend our cause, believing that the
political process will work for at least the foreseeable future.

> All this can only justify and therefore lead to tyranny as there is no mass 
> support for armed, conservtive militias. 

Ah, we agree! However, there is a constitutional "place" for state
militias such as the Ohio Military Reserve - not the Army Reserves, by the
way - which has existed in Ohio since the mid 1800's in one form or another.
I was a member several years ago, until some politico-weenies decided we
couldn't be trusted to train for anything more technical than parade duty,
at which point I resigned from this voluntary service, as did most of the
truly level-headed members of my unit. I strongly support restoration of
real state militias who do real training and are indeed "well regulated"
in the sense that they are trained how to use weapons safely, sanely and
with due regard for the damage they can cause.

It is worth noting that shortly thereafter, we were invited to join a
regional militia which had no affiliation with legitimate government. The
same level-headed group declined the invitation. 

> Apparently, this fad was started by an call by one Linda Thompson, an 
> investigator the WACO mess, who called for an armed march on Washington 
> to arrest the traitors in office.

More to agree about. According to what I've read, she is suspected of
faking video footage of the Waco incident whereby it appeared the tanks
were shooting flames into the building. When that became discredited, she
changed her story, saying she observed black-suited men setting the fire
outside the compound and the tanks were poking holes in the building to
assure fire-spreading ventilation. As far as I can tell, the legitimate
Second Amendment groups want nothing to do with her and her antics.

> In my book, Linda Thompson is suspect as an agent provacateur. 

I and a lot of other serious pro-Second Amendment folks agree. The
question is, for whom? And the implication I wish to convey to you is to
make sure you don't unwittingly place yourself in the same light.

As a side note, would you like to discuss, debate, etc., my opinion that
if the political process does not work, we will instead see the
Quebecois-zation of the US into probably three separate entities
(countries), two coastal and urban and one inland and rural? If such
movement becomes widely considered, will those inside the beltway or on
the coasts be likely to force the union to stay together?
